    '@dif': { title: 'Differential equations and waves', desc: 'The mathematics of change: equations whose unknown is a function. Almost all of physics is written with them.' },
    '@geo': { title: 'Advanced geometry: curvature and shape', desc: 'In «Geometry of the plane and of space» everything was measured with Euclid\'s rules in a flat world. Here those rules are questioned: what happens if parallels meet, how you measure how much something bends, and what is left of a figure once you stop measuring.' },
    '@est': { title: 'Structures, numbers and infinity', desc: 'The most abstract face, and the one holding up cryptography: what mathematical objects have in common, and what it means to count the uncountable.' },
    '@dis': { title: 'Discrete and computational mathematics', desc: 'The mathematics of what gets counted and of what a computer computes: networks, recurrences, algorithms, information and decisions.' },
    '@cib': { title: 'Cybernetics', desc: 'The discipline that brings together the derivative, entropy, probability and dynamical systems to answer a single question —how anything stays where it should in a world that pushes it— and out of which came automatic control and artificial intelligence.' },
    '@maq': { title: 'Machines and languages', desc: 'A computer understands nothing: it is truth tables stacked up. And a programming language is not magic: it is a text that another program translates. Both are built here, from the bottom up, until the second runs on the first.' },
    '@son': { title: 'Sound synthesis', desc: 'A sound is a function of time, and a three-line formula already makes a sound. From the wave to the note, from the note to timbre and from timbre to music: the sine, the logarithm, Fourier and recurrent sequences, heard on a synthesiser that is programmed the way a shader is.' },
    '@gfx': { title: 'Graphics programming', desc: 'Three-line rules that produce images that do not fit in your head. The mathematics of this course —the solid geometry of the final year included— drawing itself sixty times a second.' },
    '@cr': { title: 'Cryptography', desc: 'Keeping a secret in front of someone who wants it. From Caesar\'s disc to elliptic curves and to what will survive a quantum computer, with the modular arithmetic, matrices, probability and polynomials of this course doing real work: every cipher is broken and repaired right here.' },
    '@ia1': { title: 'Artificial intelligence I: learning from data', desc: 'What it means for a machine to learn: a model with parameters, a measure of how badly it is doing, and a gradient that corrects them. From classifying by distance to a network trained, regularised and evaluated honestly on what it does not know.' },
    '@ia2': { title: 'Artificial intelligence II: the architectures', desc: 'Fourteen architectures and the mathematical idea each one contributes, running small inside your browser. From sharing weights across an image to weighting with a dot product, generating by undoing noise, and learning from a reward that arrives late.' },

